<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Volsinii</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 264 BCE (approx.) · capital Volsinii</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Leading Etruscan city whose shrine of Voltumna was the religious heart of the Etruscan League, crushed by Rome in 264 BCE after a slave uprising.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Volsinii, Etruscan <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/felsina">Velzna</a>, grew on the volcanic plateau of modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-orvieto">Orvieto</a> from Villanovan settlements of the early 1st millennium BCE. By the 6th century BCE it was a wealthy city with orderly necropolises such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crocifisso-del-tufo">Crocifisso del Tufo</a>, where tombs were laid out in streets and inscribed with owners' names.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Volsinii controlled a rich territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-bolsena">Lake Bolsena</a> and traded widely, as imported Greek pottery shows. Nearby lay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fanum-voltumnae">Fanum Voltumnae</a>, the federal sanctuary where the twelve Etruscan peoples met each year for games, cult and council. The city was governed by an aristocracy; ancient sources say it was so rich that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> took some 2,000 statues as booty from it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 4th and early 3rd centuries, Volsinii's aristocrats asked Rome for help when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> and former slaves seized power. Rome besieged and destroyed the city in 264 BCE and moved survivors to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsena">Volsinii Novi</a>, modern Bolsena. Rome also transferred the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vertumnus">Vertumnus</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aventine-hill">Aventine</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/volsinii.jpg" alt="Volsinii" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bolsena.jpg" target="_blank" rel="